.Dd May 24, 2026
.Dt ARK 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ark
.Nd create and extract crash-safe archives
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm ark
.Cm create
.Bk -words
.Op Fl -hash Ar blake3 Ns | Ns Ar sha256
.Op Fl -fast
.Op Fl -verbose
.Ar archive.ark
.Ar path
.Ek
.Nm ark
.Cm extract
.Bk -words
.Op Fl -output Ar dir
.Op Fl -member Ar path
.Op Fl -overwrite
.Op Fl -verbose
.Ar archive.ark
.Ek
.Nm ark
.Cm list
.Bk -words
.Op Fl -verbose
.Op Fl -human
.Ar archive.ark
.Ek
.Nm ark
.Cm verify
.Bk -words
.Op Fl -member Ar path
.Op Fl -verbose
.Ar archive.ark
.Ek
.Nm ark
.Cm generate-reader
.Op Ar output.c
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility creates, extracts, lists, verifies, and generates recovery readers for
.Pa .ark
archives.
A
.Pa .ark
archive is a binary archive format with integrated Deflate compression,
per-member hashes, an index hash, and crash-safe writes.
.Pp
The archive format stores regular files, directories, symbolic links, and
hardlinks.
Regular file contents are split into fixed 1MB chunks, and each chunk is
compressed independently.
The archive index is verified before extraction creates filesystem objects.
.Pp
The commands are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width generate-reader
.It Cm create Oo Fl -hash Ar blake3 Ns | Ns Ar sha256 Oc Oo Fl -fast Oc Oo Fl -verbose Oc Ar archive.ark Ar path
Create
.Ar archive.ark
from
.Ar path .
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -hash Ar blake3 Ns | Ns Ar sha256
Select the hash algorithm.
The default is BLAKE3.
.It Fl -fast
Use faster Deflate parsing with a lower compression ratio.
.It Fl -verbose
Print each member path as it is added.
.El
.It Cm extract Oo Fl -output Ar dir Oc Oo Fl -member Ar path Oc Oo Fl -overwrite Oc Oo Fl -verbose Oc Ar archive.ark
Extract members from
.Ar archive.ark .
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -output Ar dir
Extract into
.Ar dir .
The default is the current directory.
.It Fl -member Ar path
Extract only the named member.
This option may be specified more than once.
If a selected hardlink requires its target member, the target is included
automatically.
.It Fl -overwrite
Overwrite existing extraction targets.
Without this option,
.Nm
checks conflicts before touching the filesystem and aborts if any target exists.
.It Fl -verbose
Print each member path as it is extracted.
.El
.It Cm list Oo Fl -verbose Oc Oo Fl -human Oc Ar archive.ark
List members in
.Ar archive.ark .
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -verbose
Print full metadata for each member.
.It Fl -human
Print sizes in human-readable form with
.Fl -verbose .
Without
.Fl -verbose ,
this option produces a warning.
.El
.It Cm verify Oo Fl -member Ar path Oc Oo Fl -verbose Oc Ar archive.ark
Verify member data and hashes in
.Ar archive.ark .
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -member Ar path
Verify only the named member.
This option may be specified more than once.
If a selected hardlink requires its target member for data verification, the
target is included automatically.
.It Fl -verbose
Print each member path as it is verified.
.El
.It Cm generate-reader Op Ar output.c
Write a standalone C11 recovery reader.
If
.Ar output.c
is omitted, the output path is
.Pa recovery.c
in the current directory.
.El
.Sh FORMAT
An archive contains, in order, a 16-byte fixed header, compressed member data,
the index, and a 64-byte footer.
The footer stores the index offset.
The index stores member metadata, data offsets, compressed sizes, chunk sizes,
and hashes.
All multi-byte integer fields are little-endian.
.Sh PLATFORM REQUIREMENTS
On Linux,
.Nm
requires kernel 5.13 or newer for Landlock filesystem sandboxing.
There is no unsandboxed fallback.
On
.Ox ,
.Nm
requires
.Ox
6.4 or newer for
.Xr unveil 2
and also uses
.Xr pledge 2 .
.Pp
The supported architectures are x86_64 and ARM64.
